s. In dimension $d = 2$, we give a combinatorial characterization in the special case when the the number of edge orbits is the minimum possible for ultrarigidity. All our results apply to a fully flexible, fixed area, or fixed periodicity lattice.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFace enumeration on simplicial complexes (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=a51f48a58e611aebd175132f75e31b8e8dc87b15&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn0search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Presently, Gal’s conjecture is known to hold for all ﬂag Q-homology 3-spheres ... [67, Problem 6.4] Γ is ﬂag and γ(∆) = f(Γ ). ... jecture is, in general, stronger than the second is due to a result of Frohmader [21],\nwhich states that for every ﬂag simplicial complex, there exists a balanced simplicial\nh-vectors of ﬂag d-polytopes (or ﬂag homology (d −1)-spheres).\nAt present, this conjecture is completely open, even in the polytopal case.\n5.2.2 Attempting to characterize f-vectors of ﬂag complexes\nWhile Gal’s conjecture can be viewed as a ﬂag analog of the GLBC, the following\nincreasingly strong conjectures of Nevo and Petersen [67] can be viewed as ﬂag\nanalogs of the necessity part of the g-conjecture.\nConjecture 7. Let ∆be a ﬂag homology sphere. Then there exists a simplicial com-\nplex Γ such that\n1. [67, Conjecture 1.4] γ(∆) = f(Γ );\n2. [67, Conjecture 6.3] Γ is balanced and γ(∆) = f(Γ );\n3. [67, Problem 6.4] Γ is ﬂag and γ(∆) = f(Γ ).\nIt follows from [22] that up to dimension four, the second and the third part of\nConjecture 7 hold and are equivalent. The reason that the third part of this con-\njecture is, in general, stronger than the second is due to a result of Frohmader [21],\nwhich states that for every ﬂag simplicial complex, there exists a balanced simplicial\ncomplex with the same f-vector.\nNevo, Petersen, and Tenner [68] proved that the version of this conjecture asking\nthat Γ should be balanced holds when 